obal", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-26T00:48:59.679Z", "request_id": "req_011CgPvhGU9K86HQCXHtDpmr", "thinking_duration_ms": 113048}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgPvhGfoCE2bKFuoiMwhP", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I pick **candidate 5**, though it beats candidate 0 only by a rough margin. I worked this out by hand, not by simulation.\n\n**1. Order quantity: candidates 0 and 5 order to the forecast; the rest over-order.** Candidate 0 orders each day's forecast exactly. Candidate 5 orders two days' forecast every other day. Candidates 3 and 6 are the daily plan scaled by about 1.1 and 1.25. Candidates 1 and 7 are the two-day plan scaled by about 1.1 and 1.2. Candidate 4 is about 1.1 with an offset. Candidate 2 orders about 1.05× every three days.\n\nLeftovers carry over to the next day, and shelf life is 5–6 days. A plan at 1.0× the forecast therefore builds a small buffer and loses almost nothing to spoilage. Lost sales come to roughly the extra ~4.6% of demand plus whatever is left at the end.\n\nRaising supply to 1.1× adds about 105 units of each product. Only about 40 of them turn into sales; the rest pile up as stock and are left over at the end.\n- **Product 1** (cost 9, a lost sale costs 14 + 2 = 16, storage 0.9 per day): about −1,000.\n- **Product 0**: about −350.\n\nSo 3 and 6 lose to 0, and 1, 7 and 4 lose to 5. Candidate 4 also has the extra order day.\n\n**2. Order frequency.** Each day with an order costs a flat 53.\n- **Daily (0, 3, 6):** 28 order days = 1,484.\n- **Every two days (1, 5, 7):** 14 days = 742. Holding a day's worth of stock overnight costs about 330 for product 0 and 452 for product 1, about 782 in total.\n- **Every three days (2):** 10 days = 530, but storage runs about 1,650 more than daily. It comes out about 700 worse, so it's out.\n\n**3. Candidate 5 vs candidate 0.** On the two fixed costs alone they are almost even: 742 saved on orders against about 782 in storage. Candidate 5 comes out ahead by about 200 for two reasons:\n- In the daily plan, a shortage on the first day of each pair is lost for good. In candidate 5 that stock is still there on the second day, so a few more units of each product get sold. That's worth about 150.\n- Its leftover buffer is a bit smaller, which saves about 100 in storage.\n\nThe two-day batches stay well inside shelf life. The 276-unit warehouse limit should only bite in rare high-stock cases.\n\nRanking: 5 > 0 > 1 ≈ 3 > 4 > 7, 6, 2."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3816, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2269, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 3816}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-26T00:49:08.673Z", "request_id": "req_011CgPvhGU9K86HQCXHtDpmr"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 2, "content_block": {"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01KhRhfEHu357p5BgXyoScmX", "name": "StructuredOutput", "input": {}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgPvhGfoCE2bKFuoiMwhP", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "cont